    <article>
      <section id="problem" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2>Day 3 and day 24 are the same balance</h2></div>
        <p>On day 3 of the month you order lunch without looking at the price. On day 24 you open your bank app twice before ordering the same lunch. Nothing changed about the money. What changed is you stopped being able to tell if you were fine.</p>
        <p>A balance cannot answer that. It tells you what is there today and nothing about how long it has to last.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="who" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">Who had the problem</p>
        <h2>Someone paid once a month, on a fixed date</h2></div>
        <p>The user is me. I have been paid a fixed salary once a month for four and a half years, and I still check my balance twice in the last week before ordering food.</p>
        <p>OneBanc gives its account to salaried people through their employer, so I could not run interviews with its users. I am saying that plainly here instead of dressing up a persona I never researched. Everything below is grounded in what OneBanc says publicly, in its own brand files, and in how seven money apps already solve the parts of this problem.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="ruledout">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">What I ruled out</p>
        <h2>Better payments loses</h2></div>
        <p>The instinct with any Indian money app is to make paying faster. That is a fight with Google Pay and Paytm, who have years of habit and dozens of services each. OneBanc would lose it.</p>
        <p>So I looked for the one thing OneBanc knows that they do not.</p>
        <blockquote>It knows when you got paid.</blockquote>
        <p>Your employer put the salary there. That date is the edge a balance never has. Once the app knows the start and end of your month, every number on the screen can be about the month.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="constraints" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">The constraints</p>
        <h2>No app, no guidelines</h2></div>
        <p>I could not get into the app. It is onboarded through HR, and I am not on a OneBanc payroll. There were no brand guidelines either.</p>
        <p>So I opened their logo files. The palette on this page comes straight out of those SVGs: a green so dark it is nearly black, and a champagne gold. Then I tested their gold on white.</p>
        <div class="ratio"><b>1.58:1</b><span>OneBanc’s gold on a white background. The minimum for readable text is 4.5:1.</span></div>
        <div class="pal" role="list" aria-label="OneBanc palette">
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#0F1F0B;color:#EEF0EA"><i>Aa</i><code>#0F1F0B</code><span>Ink</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#0F1F0B;color:#EEC97E"><i>Aa</i><code>#EEC97E</code><span>Champagne on ink</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#8F7025;color:#fff"><code>#8F7025</code><span>Gold</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#7A5F1F;color:#fff"><code>#7A5F1F</code><span>Gold ink</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#1F7A4C;color:#fff"><code>#1F7A4C</code><span>Credit</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" style="background:#6E6E6A;color:#fff"><code>#6E6E6A</code><span>Silver</span></div>
          <div role="listitem" class="fail"><i>Aa</i><span><code>#EEC97E</code> Champagne on white · 1.58:1 · Fails</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>So champagne lives only on dark surfaces, and gold text on light uses a deeper gold ink at 6.03:1. One rule, and it shaped every screen.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="arc" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The arc</p>
          <h2>What is happening, what it means, what I control</h2>
          <div class="pcol">
          <p>Three screens, one month. Home answers where you stand. Money explains where it went. Card holds the one thing OneBanc is loudest about: a card with no number printed on it.</p>
          <p>I left out a rewards screen because nothing OneBanc publishes says they run one, and designing an invented feature would undo the honesty of the rest. Settings went into Card, through their real product instead of a generic list.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="arc3">
          <div class="iph noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0"><img src="img/08-home.webp" width="804" height="2370" alt="OneBanc Home screen" loading="lazy"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:804/265;background-image:url(img/08-home.webp)"></div></div>
          <div class="iph noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0"><img src="img/09-money.webp" width="804" height="2770" alt="OneBanc Money screen" loading="lazy"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:804/253;background-image:url(img/09-money.webp)"></div></div>
          <div class="iph noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0"><img src="img/10-card.webp" width="804" height="3418" alt="OneBanc Card screen" loading="lazy"></div><div class="navfix" aria-hidden="true" style="aspect-ratio:804/230;background-image:url(img/10-card.webp)"></div></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="home" class="lt wide scr">
        
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="scrimg" src="img/08-home.webp" width="804" height="2370" alt="OneBanc Home screen showing ₹1,42,070 left this cycle" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div class="scol"><div class="sintro">
          <p class="eyebrow">Screen 1 · Home</p>
          <h2>Left this cycle, not a balance</h2>
          <p>Home is built around the salary cycle. Every block answers the question the block above it raised.</p>
        </div>
            <div class="blks">
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">01 · Where you stand</span><h3>₹1,42,070 left this cycle</h3><p>Day 12 of 30, with 18 days to the next salary. The label says Left this cycle because a balance is a number any bank shows. This one is bounded by time, which is the only thing OneBanc knows that Google Pay and Paytm do not.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">02 · What is already spoken for</span><h3>Committed before payday</h3><p>Rent and the home loan EMI, ₹18,400 together, with rent flagged Due in 2 days. This block is what makes the number above it honest. Safe to spend daily is ₹6,870 because it is what is left, minus what is committed, divided by the days to go.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">03 · What just happened</span><h3>Today, in three rows</h3><p>Swiggy, Blinkit, Rapido, with times running backwards through the day so it reads as a real Tuesday. Three rows and View all. A fourth row would be filler.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">04 · What you are leaving on the table</span><h3>3 benefits unused</h3><p>Employer linked benefits are the thing no other app in the set can offer, so they close the screen with the only gold border on it. Unused, because available is a state and unused is a loss.</p></div>
            </div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="aside">Google Pay’s home has nine sections. Paytm’s has twelve. OneBanc has four destinations, so Home has five blocks.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="money" class="lt wide scr">
        
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="scrimg" src="img/09-money.webp" width="804" height="2770" alt="OneBanc Money screen showing where ₹42,180 went" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div class="scol"><div class="sintro">
          <p class="eyebrow">Screen 2 · Money</p>
          <h2>Where ₹42,180 went</h2>
          <p>One number cut four ways, each a narrower slice of the block above it.</p>
        </div>
            <div class="blks">
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">01 · Total</span><h3>A judgement, not a receipt</h3><p>Salary credited ₹1,84,250 on 1 July, spent so far ₹42,180, over a five month chart with a dashed average line. The line underneath is the screen: ₹3,240 above your 5 month average. At this pace, ₹1,05,450 by 31 July.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">02 · By method</span><h3>₹28,400 on the card, ₹13,780 on UPI</h3><p>The same ₹42,180 cut by how you paid. The card figure is the exact number the Card screen shows in its spend meter.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">03 · By type</span><h3>Only the outlier gets gold</h3><p>Dining, Travel, Bills, Shopping and Other. Five rows that add up to ₹42,180 and 100 percent. Dining is the biggest, so it is the only gold bar. Colour means something or it means nothing.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">04 · By merchant</span><h3>18 orders says more than 32 percent</h3><p>IndiGo, Swiggy, Amazon, with counts. The counts describe behaviour without the screen having to claim anything about it.</p></div>
            </div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="card" class="lt wide scr">
        
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><img class="scrimg" src="img/10-card.webp" width="804" height="3418" alt="OneBanc Card screen with the card showing last four digits only" loading="lazy"></figure>
            <div class="scol"><div class="sintro">
          <p class="eyebrow">Screen 3 · Card</p>
          <h2>A card with nothing printed on it</h2>
          <p>OneBanc’s card has no number on the plastic. Their FAQ says it on purpose. So the screen is built around the one place those details live.</p>
        </div>
            <div class="blks">
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">01 · The card</span><h3>Last four only</h3><p>•• •••• 4021 and a name. Printing sixteen fake digits on the screen built to celebrate a numberless card would undo the whole idea.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">02 · Reveal</span><h3>Number, CVV and expiry live only here</h3><p>Every card app treats reveal as a convenience. Here it is the reason the card exists, so it gets a real button. Reveal, because it names what happens. View is what you do to a list.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">03 · Card spend</span><h3>₹28,400 of ₹75,000</h3><p>38 percent, resetting in 18 days, the same clock as Home’s next salary. Four of the reference card apps use this meter, so I kept the pattern and dropped everything credit about it. A debit card on a salary account has no bill and no due date.</p></div>
              <div class="blk"><span class="k">04 · Controls and the emergency</span><h3>Freeze is a tile, report is a decision</h3><p>Controls, Statements, Limits and Freeze sit as tiles, because freezing is routine and reversible. Report lost or stolen sits at the bottom with its consequence spelled out: Blocks the card permanently.</p></div>
            </div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="system" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The design system</p>
          <h2>Built so the typeface can still change</h2>
          <p>With no brand guidelines, the typeface was a live question. So it is a variable with four modes. Switch the mode and all three screens change together.</p>
        </div>
        <table class="tbl" style="margin-top:40px">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">Display</th><th scope="col">Text</th><th scope="col">What the variable handles</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Roboto</td><td data-l="Display">Roboto</td><td data-l="Text">Roboto</td><td>Matches OneBanc’s website</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Satoshi</td><td data-l="Display">Satoshi</td><td data-l="Text">Satoshi</td><td>Has no SemiBold, so SemiBold resolves to Bold</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Switzer</td><td data-l="Display">Switzer</td><td data-l="Text">Switzer</td><td>The widest weight range</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stack Sans</td><td data-l="Display">Stack Sans Headline</td><td data-l="Text">Stack Sans Text</td><td>Active. True optical sizes for big and small text</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="fonts" style="margin-top:24px">
          <div><b class="f-rob">₹1,42,070</b><span>Roboto</span></div>
          <div><b class="f-sat">₹1,42,070</b><span>Satoshi</span></div>
          <div><b class="f-swi">₹1,42,070</b><span>Switzer</span></div>
          <div><b class="f-sta">₹1,42,070</b><span>Stack Sans</span></div>
        </div>
        <img class="found" src="img/11-foundations.webp" width="1600" height="3427" alt="OneBanc Foundations page: colour tokens, type scale, spacing and the typeface variable" loading="lazy">
        <p class="foundline" style="margin-top:20px">Eleven colour tokens, one type scale, Material Symbols Rounded icons, and components shared across all three screens.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="copy" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">Interface copy</p>
        <h2>Every label says what the number means</h2></div>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Used</th><th scope="col">Rejected</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Left this cycle</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Available balance</td><td>Bounded by time, which is the whole concept</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Committed before payday</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Upcoming payments</td><td>Committed means already spoken for</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Safe to spend daily</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Daily budget</td><td>A verdict you can act on</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Today</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Recent transactions</td><td>One word, declares the scope</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3 benefits unused</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Your benefits</td><td>The count gives you a reason to tap</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reveal</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">View</td><td>Names what actually happens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Blocks the card permanently</td><td class="no" data-l="Rejected">Report your card</td><td>States the consequence before the tap</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section id="check" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">Cross screen check</p>
        <h2>Add any two numbers and they hold</h2></div>
        <div class="sums">
          <div><span class="n">01</span><b>₹1,84,250 − ₹42,180 = ₹1,42,070</b><p>Salary minus spend on Money equals what Home shows as left</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">02</span><b>₹28,400 on Money = ₹28,400 on Card</b><p>The same card spend on two screens</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">03</span><b>(₹1,42,070 − ₹18,400) ÷ 18 = ₹6,870</b><p>Safe to spend daily on Home</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>IndiGo at ₹6,880 and Amazon at ₹2,340 appear on both Money and Card. Swiggy is on Money but missing from Card activity, which quietly says it was paid by UPI.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="wrong" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">What I got wrong</p>
        <h2>Three versions I threw away</h2></div>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">1</span><h3>Money asserted something it could not show</h3><p>A line read Dining is your biggest jump. It was a claim, not data, so I cut it and showed merchant counts instead.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">2</span><h3>I designed the card before looking at card apps</h3><p>My first Card screen was a card graphic on a toggle list. Then I opened CRED, Kiwi, HDFC PIXEL and Uni GoldX, and none of them put toggles on the card screen. I rebuilt it around reveal, spend and services.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">3</span><h3>My own sample data contradicted itself</h3><p>Card showed ₹28,400 spent by day 12 against a ₹50,000 limit. At that pace the card stops working on day 21, a week before payday. A reviewer adding two numbers would have caught it, so I raised the limit to ₹75,000.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="changed" class="lt">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">What changed</p>
        <h2>The same figure, two different jobs</h2></div>
        <div class="ba">
          <figure class="b"><span class="k">Before</span><div class="clip"><img src="img/15a-before.webp" width="804" height="1400" alt="Plain wireframe of a normal banking home showing ₹1,42,070 as available balance" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>₹1,42,070 is the end of the answer. It tells you what is there.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="a"><span class="k">After</span><div class="clip"><img src="img/08a-home-hero.webp" width="804" height="788" alt="The top of the OneBanc Home screen, ₹1,42,070 left this cycle" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>₹1,42,070 is the start of one. It tells you how long it has to last.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <span class="k sub">How I would measure it</span>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">1</span><p>Balance checks in the last week of the cycle fall, where today they rise</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">2</span><p>Four in five people read the Home screen correctly within five seconds</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">3</span><p>Fewer overdrafts against the same person’s previous three cycles</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="learned">
        <div class="lhead"><p class="eyebrow">What I learned</p>
        <h2>Their own words were enough to design from</h2></div>
        <p>Without access to the product, the only evidence I had was the company’s own words and its own files. That turned out to be enough to design from, as long as I refused to invent anything they had not claimed.</p>
        <p>The other lesson came from my own numbers. A screen can look finished and still disagree with the screen next to it. Checking the arithmetic across all three caught more problems than any visual review did.</p>
        <p class="k sub">Still open</p>
        <p>Safe to spend daily is the boldest claim in the design. I would test whether people trust it before trusting it myself. Money’s card spend jumps from about ₹41,000 a month to a ₹71,000 pace, which is plausible if spending moved from UPI to the card, but it needs a sentence of explanation that the screen does not give yet.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
